:: SpriteScene [script]
/* ---- THE ROOM DIORAMA — an isometric sprite scene above a room's prose.

   Sewer Demons is a prose game, so this is deliberately not a viewport you steer.
   It is a small painted box at the top of the passage that says what KIND of place
   this is before you read a word of it: what the floor is, what the walls are made
   of, what is lying about. The prose still does the work; the picture sets the key.

   The art, the projection and the placement rules are all Animal Factory Tactics'
   (see art/SPRITES.md). Its tile is 56x28 — a 2:1 isometric diamond — and its props
   are drawn to a box of (w+h)*25 by (w+h)*11 + 18, which is what keeps a barrel and
   a doorway the same world. tools/prepare-sprites.py bakes those numbers into
   setup.spriteData; this file only reads them. Do not retune a scale here: that
   would put the two halves of one system out of step, and the drift would be
   invisible until a barrel stood taller than a door.

   Scenes are DERIVED, not authored. 245 rooms is too many to dress by hand and a
   half-dressed map reads worse than an undressed one, so every room gets a scene
   from three things it already publishes — its layer (the material palette), its
   role (what a room for fighting has lying in it that a room for resting does not),
   and a hash of its own name (the variation). setup.SCENE_ROOM overrides the rooms
   that have earned a specific look.

   Lives in this raw-JS script-block rather than the StoryInit run-block for the same
   reason setup.mapView does: TwineScript desugaring mangles operator-words inside
   string literals, and this file is full of them.
   ---- */

/* ---- projection ---------------------------------------------------------------
   Tile coordinates are integers at tile CENTRES; a tile covers the corner square
   (x-0.5, y-0.5)..(x+0.5, y+0.5). Walls sit on corner lines, which is why the edge
   routine below is handed half-coordinates. */
setup.SCENE_TW = 28;   /* half a tile wide  */
setup.SCENE_TH = 14;   /* half a tile tall  */

setup.sceneProject = function (x, y) {
    return { x: (x - y) * setup.SCENE_TW, y: (x + y) * setup.SCENE_TH };
};

/* ---- a per-room random that does NOT touch the game's own ---------------------
   State.random() drives loot, encounters and corruption. Drawing a picture must
   never consume a number from it, or every roll downstream of the first room you
   look at shifts. So: a string hash seeds a private 32-bit generator, and the same
   room name always dresses itself the same way, in this life and the next. */
setup.sceneRng = function (seed) {
    var h = 2166136261;
    for (var i = 0; i < seed.length; i++) {
        h ^= seed.charCodeAt(i);
        h = (h * 16777619) >>> 0;
    }
    return function () {
        h ^= h << 13; h >>>= 0;
        h ^= h >>> 17;
        h ^= h << 5;  h >>>= 0;
        return h / 4294967296;
    };
};

/* ---- material palette by layer ------------------------------------------------
   What the floor and the walls are made of, layer by layer. The sewer bands are
   built things (poured concrete, Victorian brick); the carnival is hell's own
   shanty-town, so it is sheet metal over glazed tile; the deep is ash and gravel. */
setup.SCENE_LAYER = {
    1: { ground: "ground-asphalt",  wall: "wall-brick"      },  /* storm drains — the street is the ceiling */
    2: { ground: "ground-concrete", wall: "wall-concrete"   },  /* trunk mains — the works proper */
    3: { ground: "ground-dirt",     wall: "wall-brick"      },  /* old drains — cut-and-cover, older hands */
    4: { ground: "river-water",     wall: "wall-brick"      },  /* drowned galleries — standing water */
    5: { ground: "ground-concrete", wall: "wall-concrete"   },  /* hellmouth — the threshold, still built */
    6: { ground: "ground-tiles",    wall: "wall-corrugated" },  /* the Shambles — a carnival in sheet metal */
    7: { ground: "ground-dirt",     wall: "wall-brick"      },  /* the Belly — it is not masonry any more */
    8: { ground: "ground-concrete", wall: "wall-corrugated" },  /* rendering works — a factory floor */
    9: { ground: "ground-gravel",   wall: "wall-concrete"   }   /* sulphur deep — ash and crust */
};

/* ---- the grade -----------------------------------------------------------------
   The imported art was painted for a daylit tactical map: a farm at noon. Dropped
   into this game untouched it reads as a clean warehouse, which is the one thing a
   sewer is not. So every scene gets a wash keyed to its BAND -- the same three-way
   split the map already uses (setup.mapMeta.band).

   It is applied with "source-atop", which tints only pixels that were actually drawn
   and leaves the empty corners alone. A plain multiply over the whole rect would
   paint the transparent background too and give every scene a hard rectangular edge,
   which is exactly what the vignette is there to avoid. */
setup.SCENE_GRADE = {
    sewer:   "rgba(60, 102, 74, 0.34)",    /* cold, wet, algal — a working sewer */
    between: "rgba(62, 80, 118, 0.34)",    /* colder and bluer — drowned, and further from the sun */
    hell:    "rgba(152, 72, 42, 0.34)",    /* rust and firelight */
    meta:    "rgba(60, 102, 74, 0.28)"
};
/* Each wash is a SATURATED MID-TONE, not a dark one, and that is the whole trick.
   source-atop blends toward the wash colour, so the result carries the wash's
   luminance as well as its hue: the first two passes used near-black greens and rusts,
   which coloured the scene correctly and dragged it to a smear at the same time
   (measured mean luma fell from 111 to 76, and a room of jail bars became unreadable).
   These sit at roughly the luminance of the art itself, so they shift the KEY without
   touching the exposure, and the vignette and the page's black surround are left to
   carry the dark. Check with the luma numbers, not by eye -- a dark page makes
   everything on it look fine. */

/* what tends to be lying about, by what the room is FOR */
setup.SCENE_ROLE_PROPS = {
    room:   ["crate-wood", "barrel-single", "pallet", "barrels-cluster", "crate-steel", "spare-parts"],
    rest:   ["crate-wood", "table-wood", "barrel-single", "first-aid-kit", "supply-chest-closed", "toolbox-closed"],
    fight:  ["sandbags", "crate-stack", "barrels-cluster", "pallet", "spare-parts"],
    lander: ["sandbags", "crate-stack", "barrel-single", "spare-parts"],
    ending: ["barrel-single", "crate-stack"]
};

/* ---- rooms that have earned a specific look -----------------------------------
   Anything named here replaces or extends the derived scene. Keep this list short
   and meaningful: a room belongs here when the prose has already promised the
   player something they should be able to SEE. */
setup.SCENE_ROOM = {
    TheSurgery:       { props: ["medical-surgical-table", "instrument-trolley", "medicine-cabinet"] },
    TheFleshcutter:   { props: ["medical-exam-table", "instrument-trolley"] },
    TheWard:          { props: ["hospital-bed", "iv-stand", "bedside-monitor"] },
    TheHolding:       { wall: "jail-bars", props: ["pallet", "crate-wood"] },
    TheCageAct:       { wall: "jail-bars", props: ["crate-stack"] },
    TheBirthingDens:  { wall: "jail-bars", props: ["pallet", "crate-wood"] },
    TheBreeder:       { wall: "jail-bars", props: ["pallet"] },
    TheSty:           { wall: "jail-bars", props: ["pallet", "barrel-single"] },
    TheStyCleared:    { wall: "jail-bars", props: ["pallet", "barrel-single"] },
    /* shadowed today by a hand-drawn roomart, and kept on purpose: if that
       illustration is ever replaced the derived scene should still be the right one.
       tools/scene-probe.mjs reports it as SHADOWED rather than as a fault. */
    TheBathhouse:     { ground: "ground-tiles", props: ["scrub-sink", "crate-wood"] },
    TheDrumStore:     { props: ["barrels-cluster", "barrel-single", "crate-stack", "pallet"] },
    TheGaugeHouse:    { props: ["workbench-vise", "lab-control-console", "toolbox-open"] },
    ThePumpRoom:      { props: ["workbench-metal", "lab-control-console", "barrel-single"] },
    TheValvePuzzle:   { props: ["lab-control-console", "workbench-metal"] },
    TheVentControl:   { props: ["lab-control-console", "toolbox-closed"] },
    Maintenance:      { props: ["workbench-metal", "toolbox-open", "spare-parts", "wire-cutters"] },
    TheSmithStall:    { props: ["workbench-vise", "crate-steel", "barrel-single"] },
    TheButchersStall: { props: ["table-steel", "crate-wood"] },
    TheStillborn:     { props: ["wheeled-stretcher", "iv-stand"] },
    TheColdStore:     { props: ["crate-steel", "crate-stack", "barrels-cluster"] },
    TheLostAndFound:  { props: ["crate-wood", "wooden-crate-open", "supply-chest-open", "crate-stack"] },
    TheStill:         { props: ["barrels-cluster", "barrel-single", "table-wood"] },
    TheLarder:        { props: ["crate-wood", "supply-chest-closed", "table-wood"] }
};

/* ---- the sprite cache ---------------------------------------------------------
   Images load asynchronously and a passage renders once, so every scene registers
   a redraw for when its art arrives. */
setup.sceneImages = {};

/* An image is only safe to draw when BOTH flags are up. naturalWidth goes non-zero as
   soon as the header parses, which is BEFORE the pixels have decoded; drawImage in that
   window silently draws nothing and returns normally. Guarding on naturalWidth alone
   baked a permanently blank floor tile into the cache the first time a redraw fired
   while the ground texture was mid-decode -- walls and props only escaped it by winning
   the race. Ask for complete as well, always. */
setup.sceneReady = function (img) { return !!(img && img.complete && img.naturalWidth); };

setup.sceneImage = function (file, onLoad) {
    var cache = setup.sceneImages;
    if (!cache[file]) {
        var img = new Image();
        img.dataset.ready = "";
        img.addEventListener("load", function () { img.dataset.ready = "1"; });
        img.src = "img/sprite/" + file;
        cache[file] = img;
    }
    var im = cache[file];
    if (onLoad && !im.dataset.ready) { im.addEventListener("load", onLoad, { once: true }); }
    return im;
};

/* the ground texture is a square that has to lie down on a diamond. Skew it once
   into an offscreen canvas at 2x and let drawImage do the downsample — the same
   trick Tactics uses, and it is the difference between a crisp floor and a stairstep. */
setup.SCENE_GROUND_SS = 4;   /* supersample: build the diamond at 4x the tile and let
                                drawImage downsample it. At 1x the floor stairsteps
                                along every diamond edge; at 4x it does not, and the
                                cost is one 224x112 canvas per material, once. */
setup.sceneGroundCache = {};
setup.sceneGroundTile = function (id, img) {
    var cache = setup.sceneGroundCache;
    if (!cache[id] && setup.sceneReady(img)) {
        var k = setup.SCENE_GROUND_SS, w = setup.SCENE_TW * 2 * k, h = setup.SCENE_TH * 2 * k;
        var c = document.createElement("canvas");
        c.width = w; c.height = h;
        var g = c.getContext("2d");
        /* skew the square texture onto the diamond: its four corners land on the
           diamond's four corners, which is what makes a flat tile lie down */
        g.setTransform(w / 2 / img.naturalWidth, h / 2 / img.naturalHeight,
                       -w / 2 / img.naturalWidth, h / 2 / img.naturalHeight, w / 2, 0);
        g.drawImage(img, 0, 0);
        cache[id] = c;
    }
    return cache[id];
};

/* ---- derive a scene from a room name ------------------------------------------ */
setup.sceneFor = function (name) {
    var layer = setup.depthOf ? setup.depthOf[name] : null;
    if (!layer || !setup.SCENE_LAYER[layer]) { return null; }

    /* the room's role, straight off the baked map graph */
    var role = "room", G = setup.mapGraph && setup.mapGraph[String(layer)];
    if (G) {
        for (var i = 0; i < G.rooms.length; i++) {
            if (G.rooms[i][0] === name) { role = G.rooms[i][1]; break; }
        }
    }

    var rng = setup.sceneRng(name);
    var mat = setup.SCENE_LAYER[layer];
    var over = setup.SCENE_ROOM[name] || {};
    var spec = {
        w: 4 + Math.floor(rng() * 3),          /* 4..6 tiles across */
        h: 3 + Math.floor(rng() * 2),          /* 3..4 tiles deep   */
        ground: over.ground || mat.ground,
        wall: over.wall || mat.wall,
        band: (setup.mapMeta && setup.mapMeta.band[String(layer)]) || "sewer",
        role: role,
        props: []
    };

    /* Place props on the BACK half only. The front row is where a reader's eye
       lands and where an actor will stand once there is character art, so it stays
       clear — a prop there would have to be occluded by a figure that does not
       exist yet, and the empty foreground reads as floor rather than as a gap. */
    var pool = (over.props || setup.SCENE_ROLE_PROPS[role] || setup.SCENE_ROLE_PROPS.room).slice();
    var slots = [];
    for (var y = 0; y < Math.max(1, spec.h - 1); y++) {
        for (var x = 0; x < spec.w; x++) { slots.push([x, y]); }
    }
    /* Fisher-Yates on the private generator */
    for (var s = slots.length - 1; s > 0; s--) {
        var j = Math.floor(rng() * (s + 1));
        var t = slots[s]; slots[s] = slots[j]; slots[j] = t;
    }

    /* scale with the floor, not a flat count: two crates that furnish a 4x3 leave a
       6x4 looking swept. Roughly one prop per eight tiles, plus a coin-flip. */
    var want = over.props ? pool.length : 2 + Math.floor((spec.w * spec.h) / 8) + Math.floor(rng() * 2);
    var taken = {};
    for (var p = 0; p < pool.length && spec.props.length < want; p++) {
        var id = over.props ? pool[p] : pool[Math.floor(rng() * pool.length)];
        var def = setup.spriteData.props[id];
        if (!def) { continue; }
        var fw = def.footprint[0], fh = def.footprint[1];
        for (var k = 0; k < slots.length; k++) {
            var sx = slots[k][0], sy = slots[k][1], free = true;
            if (sx + fw > spec.w || sy + fh > spec.h) { continue; }
            for (var cx = sx; cx < sx + fw && free; cx++) {
                for (var cy = sy; cy < sy + fh && free; cy++) {
                    if (taken[cx + "," + cy]) { free = false; }
                }
            }
            if (!free) { continue; }
            for (var mx = sx; mx < sx + fw; mx++) {
                for (var my = sy; my < sy + fh; my++) { taken[mx + "," + my] = 1; }
            }
            spec.props.push({ id: id, x: sx, y: sy });
            break;
        }
    }
    return spec;
};

/* ---- draw ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Painter's order: floor, then the two back walls, then everything standing up in
   depth order (x+y ascending), so a nearer barrel covers a further one. */
setup.SCENE_HEAD = 80;      /* headroom above the floor: a 72px wall plus a little overhang */
setup.SCENE_TARGET_H = 300; /* how tall the picture sits on the page, in CSS pixels.

   Zoom is derived from THIS and not passed in, because an isometric floor is
   inherently half as tall as it is wide: fixing the width instead would make a
   column-wide scene 280px of floor before a single wall went up, and a big room
   would tower over a small one for no reason the player could read. Pinning the
   HEIGHT makes every room the same weight on the page -- a plate of a consistent
   size, the way the hand-drawn room illustrations already are -- and lets the width
   say how big the room is. */

setup.spriteScene = function (host, spec) {
    if (!host || !spec || !setup.spriteData) { return; }
    var TW = setup.SCENE_TW, TH = setup.SCENE_TH, D = setup.spriteData;
    var HEAD = setup.SCENE_HEAD;
    var w = spec.w, h = spec.h;
    var zoom = setup.SCENE_TARGET_H / ((w + h) * TH + HEAD);
    var cw = (w + h) * TW * zoom;
    var ch = setup.SCENE_TARGET_H;
    var dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, 2);

    var canvas = host.querySelector("canvas");
    if (!canvas) { canvas = document.createElement("canvas"); host.appendChild(canvas); }
    canvas.width = Math.round(cw * dpr);
    canvas.height = Math.round(ch * dpr);
    /* draw 1:1 in CSS pixels (dpr only raises the backing store), so nothing is
       resampled a second time by the browser */
    canvas.style.width = Math.round(cw) + "px";
    canvas.style.height = Math.round(ch) + "px";

    var ctx = canvas.getContext("2d");
    var redraw = function () { setup.spriteScene(host, spec); };
    ctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, cw, ch);
    ctx.save();
    ctx.translate(h * TW * zoom, (TH + HEAD) * zoom);

    var proj = function (x, y) {
        var p = setup.sceneProject(x, y);
        return { x: p.x * zoom, y: p.y * zoom };
    };

    /* floor */
    var gdef = D.grounds[spec.ground];
    if (gdef) {
        var gimg = setup.sceneImage(gdef.file, redraw);
        var tile = setup.sceneGroundTile(spec.ground, gimg);
        if (tile) {
            for (var gy = 0; gy < h; gy++) {
                for (var gx = 0; gx < w; gx++) {
                    var q = proj(gx, gy);
                    ctx.drawImage(tile, q.x - TW * zoom, q.y - TH * zoom, TW * 2 * zoom, TH * 2 * zoom);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    /* the two far walls, along the corner lines y = -0.5 and x = -0.5 */
    var edef = D.edges[spec.wall];
    if (edef) {
        var eimg = setup.sceneImage(edef.file, redraw);
        if (setup.sceneReady(eimg)) {
            for (var ex = 0; ex < w; ex++) { setup.sceneEdge(ctx, edef, eimg, proj, ex - 0.5, -0.5, ex + 0.5, -0.5, zoom); }
            for (var ey = h - 1; ey >= 0; ey--) { setup.sceneEdge(ctx, edef, eimg, proj, -0.5, ey + 0.5, -0.5, ey - 0.5, zoom); }
        }
    }

    /* everything standing on the floor, far to near */
    var standing = spec.props.map(function (p) {
        var def = D.props[p.id];
        return def ? { p: p, def: def, depth: p.x + p.y + (def.footprint[0] + def.footprint[1]) / 2 } : null;
    }).filter(Boolean);
    standing.sort(function (a, b) { return a.depth - b.depth; });
    standing.forEach(function (it) { setup.sceneProp(ctx, it.def, setup.sceneImage(it.def.file, redraw), proj, it.p, zoom); });

    ctx.restore();

    /* the band wash, over everything that got drawn and nothing that did not */
    var grade = setup.SCENE_GRADE[spec.band] || setup.SCENE_GRADE.sewer;
    ctx.globalCompositeOperation = "source-atop";
    ctx.fillStyle = grade;
    ctx.fillRect(0, 0, cw, ch);
    ctx.globalCompositeOperation = "source-over";
};

/* a prop: scaled into the box Tactics calibrated for its footprint, stood on the
   bottom-centre of its tile group */
setup.sceneProp = function (ctx, def, img, proj, p, zoom) {
    if (!setup.sceneReady(img)) { return; }
    var fw = def.footprint[0], fh = def.footprint[1];
    var q = proj(p.x + (fw - 1) / 2, p.y + (fh - 1) / 2);
    var scale = Math.min(def.box[0] * zoom / img.naturalWidth, def.box[1] * zoom / img.naturalHeight);
    var dw = img.naturalWidth * scale, dh = img.naturalHeight * scale;
    ctx.save();
    ctx.translate(q.x, q.y + (fw + fh) * 5 * zoom);
    ctx.drawImage(img, -dw / 2, -dh, dw, dh);
    ctx.restore();
};

/* an edge piece: sheared so its baked baseline lands on the tile edge running from
   corner (ax,ay) to corner (bx,by). The quad in the manifest is the art's own ground
   line plus how tall it stands there, so the same routine hangs a wall, a railing,
   a door and a run of jail bars without knowing which it has. */
setup.sceneEdge = function (ctx, def, img, proj, ax, ay, bx, by, zoom) {
    var a = proj(ax, ay), b = proj(bx, by);
    var x0 = def.baseline[0], y0 = def.baseline[1], x1 = def.baseline[2], y1 = def.baseline[3];
    var srcH = def.baseline[4];
    var d = (def.height * zoom) / srcH;
    var sx = (b.x - a.x) / (x1 - x0);
    var sy = ((b.y - a.y) - d * (y1 - y0)) / (x1 - x0);
    ctx.save();
    ctx.transform(sx, sy, 0, d, a.x - sx * x0, a.y - sy * x0 - d * y0);
    ctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0);
    ctx.restore();
};


/* ---- wiring ------------------------------------------------------------------
   Every mapped room gets its diorama without a single passage being edited: a
   :passagedisplay handler renders any <<scene>> container that was written by hand,
   and otherwise prepends one itself.

   Two rooms are skipped on purpose. One that already carries a <<roomart>> keeps it —
   a drawn illustration of THAT place beats a derived box of the right KIND of place,
   and stacking both would say the same thing twice at different quality. One that is
   not in setup.depthOf is not a room at all (a menu, a combat screen, an ending), and
   sceneFor returns null for it.

   The toggle is not decoration. This is a prose horror game and a picture over every
   room changes how it is read; a player who wants the dark back should be able to
   have it. Settings persist across lives on their own. */
Setting.addToggle("sceneArt", {
    label: "Room pictures — an isometric sketch of each place, above its description.",
    default: true
});

setup.sceneRenderAll = function (root) {
    var host = root || document;
    var boxes = host.querySelectorAll(".room-scene[data-scene]");
    for (var i = 0; i < boxes.length; i++) {
        if (boxes[i].dataset.drawn) { continue; }
        var spec = setup.sceneFor(boxes[i].dataset.scene);
        if (spec) { boxes[i].dataset.drawn = "1"; setup.spriteScene(boxes[i], spec); }
        else { boxes[i].style.display = "none"; }
    }
};

$(document).on(":passagedisplay", function (ev) {
    var el = ev.content || document.querySelector("#passages .passage");
    if (!el) { return; }
    var name = (ev.passage && ev.passage.title) || passage();

    if (settings.sceneArt === false) {
        el.querySelectorAll(".room-scene").forEach(function (b) { b.style.display = "none"; });
        return;
    }
    if (!el.querySelector(".room-scene") && !el.querySelector(".room-illus") && setup.sceneFor(name)) {
        var box = document.createElement("div");
        box.className = "room-scene";
        box.dataset.scene = name;
        el.insertBefore(box, el.firstChild);
    }
    setup.sceneRenderAll(el);
});


:: SceneWidgets [widget]
/* The "scene" widget — place a room's diorama explicitly, when the automatic one is
   in the wrong spot or you want another room's picture (a look-through, a vision, a
   map preview). Call it as: scene, or scene "TheFatberg". Put it at the very top of a
   passage, above any nobr, exactly like the roomart widget. Writing one suppresses
   the automatic insert for that passage.

   NOTE the macro names above are written WITHOUT their angle brackets. This passage is
   wikified at init, and a C-style comment does not stop that: a literal container tag
   in here (nobr, silently, script) opens a block that never closes and swallows the
   widget definitions below it, which hangs the engine on "Loading..." with no error.
   Same trap as the one commit a83dab1 hardened the script-block against. */
<<widget "scene">>\
<div class="room-scene" @data-scene="_args.length ? _args[0] : passage()"></div>\
<</widget>>
